TEXT -3064 688 Left 2 ;BQ input w| FOCV modulation simulation: see previous versions
TEXT 2336 336 Left 2 ;ATT!!\nComment out CD components that I use that are not CD4013B from cd4000_v lib \nthis avoids problems from overlapping definitions
TEXT -2896 368 Left 2 ;Flip flops' supply voltage \n(their models require a source called VDD)\nThey will be supplied by VDDR in reality
TEXT -3064 264 Left 5 ;Input power + supply generation
TEXT 2344 264 Left 5 ;Simulation parameters and libraries
TEXT -800 840 Left 5 ;This section is responsible for attaching to single cap a load consuming a certain amount of energy.\nTasks are configured as a constant power load for a certain amount of time, yielding the measured \ntask energy consumption in worst case scenario (max supply voltage)
TEXT 1416 1544 Left 3 ;EOF simulates task duration by delaying POS_EDGE (EN_2 rising edge pulse) of the active task's duration. \nEOF must only be a short peak: if it stays high, we cannot set EN2 at capdyn load control side, as EOF keeps RDY in reset!
TEXT -784 1768 Left 3 ;EOFR clocks the next task shift register 50 ms after an EOF is issued (EOF is the POS_EDGE, but delayed by task time)\nNext task register must be clocked once EOF has had its effect on capdyn output control (EN2).\nFor this purpose, we delay the EOF signal by 50 ms and pilot the task register with this delayed signal, EOFR.\n \nIf we were to pilot task register directly from EOF:\nEOF would not have time to deactivate EN2, causing problems.
TEXT -3064 864 Left 3 ;This acts as the MCU's task scheduling algorithm, which sets up a circular sequence of four tasks.\nOnce EN2 is enabled, the current task executed and issues an EOF at the end of the task. Once EOF is issued, we set up the next task.\nIn reality, the MCU will control the task register in capdyn, setting up the next task in the "next task" register.\nFor the sake of the LTspice simulation, we needed the MCu's signals telling us which is the next task - this is what we have done here,\nTo see how the task register is actually implemented, see full_v2
TEXT -3080 808 Left 5 ;MCU SIMULATION
TEXT -280 1088 Left 3 ;EN2 rising edge identifies moment where load is first connected to capdyn main bank.\nThis circuit generates a 100 ms pulse immediately as EN2 rising edge is detected.
TEXT -1072 1128 Left 5 ;Next task register control
TEXT 1216 1128 Left 5 ;Task avg pow and duration simulation
TEXT 208 624 Left 4 ;We consider a load switch and a voltage monitor for attaching and detaching the load from the capacitor.\nWe draw this small infrastructural consumption directly from the single capacitor.
TEXT -984 264 Left 5 ;Single capacitor storage
TEXT 1424 1312 Left 3 ;Load average power consumption varying with matched stage.
TEXT 2928 1224 Left 2 ;Given energy for task, we can\n also fix avg power and compute\n the time required to finish it. \nCheck energy_check,m
TEXT -2992 1128 Left 5 ;MCU sim: Circular scheduling and voltage monitor simulator
TEXT -736 488 Left 2 ;Capacitor size: last stage of Capyn\nRemember, single cap does not have \nboosting feature!!
TEXT 1232 320 Left 2 ;MINIMUM INFRASTRUCTURE for intermittent operation: \n2 x 500 nA quiescent current of LTC2934 VM + 200 nA bias resistors\n20 nA x 7 CD4000 elements
TEXT -264 264 Left 5 ;Infrastructural load and load switch
TEXT 3712 608 Left 2 ;Task duration (s)
TEXT 3712 672 Left 2 ;Task avg power consumption (W)
TEXT 3712 640 Left 2 ;Task repetitions (number)
